when I first heard about the quarters I  was appalled you know I got in law  enforcement cuz I wanted to serve from  from sag not be a bully you're way  beyond encouraging productivity at this  point now you're you know you're you're  encouraging police officers to act  unlawfully Auburn Alabama is home to  sprawling plains the Auburn University  Tigers and a questionable police force  after the arrival of a new police chief  in 2010 the department entered an era of  ticket quotas a common thread tying  Auburn PD to other departments in  Alabama and even nationwide Air Force  veteran an Auburn native Justin Hannah's  was a police officer on the force who  felt compelled to speak out the role  police in society I believe are to  interfere with the lives of the people  as little as possible but protect them  from you know the 1% element that wants  to victimize them you know let them be  free to live their lives but protect the  people and the property that's that's  what they pay us to do when I first  started in 2006 everything was great  morale was high the officers we had  cookouts together worked hard and you  know we pushed each other to be our best  and you know had great relationship with  the public but in 2010 things just  immediately took a turn Auburn pd's new  police chief chief Dawson had new  priorities and new marching orders  orders that the teen of command obeyed  without objection Justin recorded  conversations between his sergeant  sergeant Neil and the other officers to  keep tabs on any foul play it came in  and you know they immediately started  pushing for tickets I will have 100  contacts per month for men on 40 of  those lady warnings for traffic doesn't  seek to be divided between traffic  citations non traffic citations  custodial arrest do not be one that  there's nothing to hungry that's 72,000  contacts a year like a 50,000 person  town one thing sergeant Neil did was the  rod and carrot method you know if you  didn't get the the number of tickets he  wanted if you didn't get the hundred  contacts you get mandatory overtime you  know you get written up but if you did  do well the officer who wrote the most  tickets and the one that came in second  we're giving gift cards like steak  dinners and things like that  quotas pervert police priorities Radley  Balko is an investigative journalist for  the Huffington Post and author of the  new book rise of the warrior cop he says  that these quotas are an invitation to  corruption and bad policing there's  gonna be a strong incentive for police  officers may be defined offenses where  they didn't exist sergeant Neil started  instructing me and my partner to arrest  people that we didn't feel like it broke  the law  it was one I think was like maybe Friday  or Saturday night one the big bar nights  we saw a guy walking on the sidewalk  he was staggering a little bit but it  was you know walking quietly to himself  he was on the sidewalk wasn't stepping  out in the street it's not well it's  clear he's been drinking and will at  least contact and make sure he's okay so  we get out on them we contact him he  knows who he is where he is where he's  going we determined that he's good to go  we let him go well our sergeant saw it  and told us to go back and arrest him he  pointed to me and said you need it for  your stats so I told him it's not about  what I need it's about what he needs and  he doesn't need to go to jail so he got  out the car and you know you know  ordered us to arrest them and I still  refused my partner  you know scared for his job you know  went ahead and arrested the guy took him  to jail for public intoxication so you  have a policy that encourages police to  create petty crimes and to ignore  serious crimes and that's I mean that's  clearly the the very opposite of we want  what we want police officers to be doing  justin repeatedly expressed his concern  about the quotas and the way things are  being handled to his entire chain of  command he followed up with a formal  grievance process of a director of  Public Safety but to no avail  the director said that these  requirements were necessary for  increasing productivity you have to  question the leadership there if  officers need to have that sort of  incentive just to perform their jobs I  think personally that is revenue driven  at the same time that they're trying to  raise property taxes at the same time  they're trying to raise sales taxes  they're pushing us to get more tickets  and they're telling us they don't want  these little fix-it tickets they tell us  that they won't quote meet tickets  something with a fine they look over  your track traffic log and if you've got  a bunch of low fun or no fine tickets  they're going to tell you to switch and  do more speeding more red lights more  more you know things like that there's  not that many speeders there's not that  many people running a red light to get  those numbers so what officers do is  lower their standards when you're  issuing citations you're forcibly taking  money from people from citizens and  you're you know you're putting points on  their driving records I mean you're  inhibiting their freedom and a lot of  ways these kind of policies that the  ticket quotas and the heavy hand  enforcement will affect the the  lower-income population is the most when  you write somebody two or three hundred  sixty-four dollar tickets in a month  they've got to make some choices that  month  that's you know that that's you know  groceries you know that's maybe food or  clothes for kids that might be you know  car house payment it wasn't long before  Justin had to start worrying about his  own finances after the department made  it clear that if he continues to speak  out it would cost him his job during the  grievance process they try to squash it  on a technicality you know they tried to  talk me out of it you know try to  buddy-buddy up with him to get it to  drop it and they once all else failed  they started threatening my job my  career warned me there'd be consequences  for doing this I had no intention of  dropping you know at this point I had no  choice I think I had to keep going  forward and if I stopped and nothing  would have change there's just been  another your minor hiccup justin was  fired and while the department declined  reason TVs requests for information on  the case Justin says he was ultimately  fired for violating a gag order when  they call me in fired me the first thing  that went through my mind was my kids  you know I'm a primary breadwinner I  don't know how the Builder gonna get  paid and you know I didn't know what I  was gonna do as far as a new career you  know all my eggs are in this law  enforcement basket knows what my calling  was it's what I've always wanted to do  justin hasn't found a new job he's  currently looking for a lawyer to take  up his case and sued the department  though after reaching out to the Police  Benevolent Association for guidance he  found out that his grievances were  actually pretty common  they're like basically here we go with  this skins it's a problem in more places  than Auburn this is a frequently  occurring thing and it's about it with a  lot of different officers and  departments well justin will continue  his battle on the legal front he thinks  that the fastest way to get the city  back on track is public accountability  once the people know they can hold their  elected officials accountable and then  it'll change you know officers have  tried for years to change it from the  inside and they're either forced out or  just leave from frustration and and what  you end up with is a police department  where the good officers have been run  off and you have a bunch of officer just  willing to play the game they want to be  the bullies you know they want to show  how bad they over the badge and that's  what you get left  [Music]  you

